  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B008",
        "laureates": [
          "Alexander A. Belavin",
          "Alexander B. Zamolodchikov",
          "Alexander M. Polyakov"
        ],
        "citation": "for establishing conformal field theory of two-dimensional critical phenomena"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B013",
    "B033"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B008 remains my preferred achievement because it identified an unusually compact and universal structure behind diverse critical systems. It did not merely provide a useful calculational technique: conformal symmetry made exact statements about two-dimensional critical phenomena possible and created a durable bridge between statistical physics and quantum field theory. The three credited names form a coherent foundational attribution, and the framework has had decades to prove both its explanatory force and its generative value.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation is attributional and conceptual: conformal field theory has several intellectual antecedents, and the boundary between the distinct contributions of the three proposed laureates must be documented carefully. Its impact is also predominantly theoretical, so the final case should make explicit why its experimentally connected universality in critical phenomena constitutes a discovery rather than solely an influential formal language.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson places B008 below more directly measurable achievements, while recognizing its profound, decades-long influence and warning that the boundaries relative to other conformal-field-theory pioneers are difficult to draw.",
        "response": "I agree that the boundary needs rigorous historical scrutiny, but regard that as a reason to sharpen the citation, not to discount the achievement. The appeal of B008 is precisely that a simple symmetry principle yielded universal, testable consequences across many critical systems; this is a particularly clear example of deep theory organizing complex physical phenomena."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues strongly for B013, emphasizing that dynamical mean-field theory transformed quantitative understanding of correlated electrons, while noting an earlier intellectual lineage and its character as a framework.",
        "response": "B013 is an excellent alternative and has the virtue of sustained contact with real materials. Yet its status as an approximation whose scope varies with the problem, together with the diffuse lineage of its formulation and extensions, makes a compact prize attribution less clean than for B008. I would keep B013 under active consideration rather than treat this as a dismissal."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll favors B033 because optical lattice clocks combine a clearly attributable concept with independently reproduced, exceptional metrological performance.",
        "response": "The maturity and attribution of B033 are indeed compelling, and it is my leading experimental alternative. I would not make an eventual SI redefinition decisive either way. My present preference for B008 rests instead on the exceptional breadth and explanatory permanence of its universal theoretical structure."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Which foundational conformal-field-theory contributions fall inside a historically defensible three-person attribution.",
      "Whether the citation can distinguish the central two-dimensional critical-phenomena achievement from the wider conformal-field-theory lineage with sufficient precision."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No connection requiring recusal is apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
